zsh: braces as zsh's lexer reads them

zsh decides what a brace is while it lexes, and the parser read three of
its rules differently:

- An unquoted } that ends a word, followed by a separator, closes a
  block unless a { earlier in the word, including in an unquoted ${...}
  in it, is still open. The parser kept it in the word, so it accepted
  "echo a}", which zsh rejects, and read "{ echo a}" as unterminated.
- A { that starts a word at command position opens a block even when
  glued, so "{echo hi}", "{a}" and "f() {echo hi 2>&1}" are blocks. The
  parser read "{echo" as a command name.
- An unquoted ${...} ends at the } that balances its braces, so
  "${x/{f}/Y}" replaces {f} and "${y//{Q}/1 2}" is one word. The parser
  closed it at the first }, which is right only inside double quotes and
  here-documents, where zsh does the same.

Reproducer, before this change:

	$ printf 'echo a}\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	echo a}
	$ printf '{ echo a}\n' | shfmt -ln zsh
	<standard input>:1:1: reached EOF without matching `{` with `}`
	$ zsh -nc 'echo a}'
	zsh:1: parse error near `}'

With LangZsh, the parser now splits such a } off arguments and redirect
targets as its own token, splits a glued { off a word at command
position, and counts the braces in the words of an unquoted ${...}. It
hands out the split tokens through a one-token pushback in the lexer,
which a parse error drops along with the current token, so that the
parser stops at the error instead of reading the pushed token
(">0}}})" looped forever in callExpr).

Part of https://github.com/mvdan/sh/issues/120.

